.TH __CHTTPSVR_DESTROY 3 "2026-07-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
__chttpsvr_destroy \- free an HTTP server
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

void __chttpsvr_destroy(chttpsvr srv);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Stops this server's listener, drains all in-flight requests for this
server (up to 30 seconds), then closes every remaining connection of
this server (idle, mid-handshake, or otherwise still active) and waits
for each to actually finish closing (up to a further 30 seconds),
releases this server's reference to the shared reactor, then frees all
routers, routes, middleware nodes, the server's owned ctpool, and the
server struct itself. Both waits are bounded escape hatches for a
pathologically stuck connection (e.g. one configured with no read
timeout at all whose peer never sends anything); neither one is hit in
ordinary operation, where every connection either completes or reaches
one of its own configured timeouts well before 30 seconds.

If this was the last reference to the shared reactor (from any
chttpsvr instance in the process), its actual teardown happens
asynchronously on a separate thread and is NOT guaranteed complete by
the time this function returns.  Call chttpsvr_engine_wait() afterward
for a synchronous guarantee (e.g. before reusing the port this server
was listening on, or before process exit).  This reactor is
independent of chttpclient's own engine, so destroying the last
chttpsvr has no effect on any chttpclient async work in the same
process.

Also calls clog_close on the server's own logger (the internal
stderr/FATAL-only logger, or the logger derived from the cl passed to
create_chttpsvr_mp / create_chttpsvr).  This never closes a
caller-supplied cl directly, since the server only ever stores a
derived copy of it.

For clean multi-server teardown, call chttpsvr_stop on all servers
first to close their listeners, then call __chttpsvr_destroy on each
handle in any order.

Safe to call on a server that was already force-stopped by
chttpsvr_engine_stop() while still running: that function quiesces
(stops listening, drains in-flight requests, closes connections,
releases the engine reference for) every chttpsvr instance still
registered in the process before the shared reactor is actually torn
down, so calling this function afterward on the same handle only frees
the remaining per-server memory (routers, routes, the server struct
itself); it never double-closes the listener, double-releases the
engine reference, or otherwise repeats work already done.

Does not set the caller's pointer to NULL; use the chttpsvr_destroy
macro for that.

Safe to call with NULL.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_destroy (3),
.BR ___chttpsvr_destroy (3),
